`Ongoing - Each Player` triggers when its conditions become true. It does not continuously rerun its actions while they stay true. Your original rule increments once on the press; releasing Interact makes the condition false and rearms it for the next press.

This complete native Workshop example keeps a separate counter for each player, initializes it on player load, increments immediately on pressing Interact, and then repeats with a requested 0.1-second pause:

```text
variables
{
    player:
        0: Ticks
}

rule("Initialize Ticks")
{
    event
    {
        Ongoing - Each Player;
        All;
        All;
    }
    actions
    {
        Event Player.Ticks = 0;
    }
}

rule("Count while Interact is held")
{
    event
    {
        Ongoing - Each Player;
        All;
        All;
    }
    conditions
    {
        Is Button Held(Event Player, Button(Interact)) == True;
    }
    actions
    {
        Modify Player Variable(Event Player, Ticks, Add, 1);
        Wait(0.1, Abort When False);
        Loop If Condition Is True;
    }
}
```

`Wait` paces the repetition. `Abort When False` ends the running action list when Interact is released during the wait. `Loop If Condition Is True` restarts the actions only while the rule's condition still holds. Releasing stops increments; pressing again resumes from the existing total.

Timing caveat: the bundled archived documentation reports that ordinary-map waits round upward to 0.016-second steps, so `Wait(0.1, ...)` takes approximately 0.112 seconds, not exactly 0.100. Practice Range timing is also reported to differ.

